# VS Code's preview tabs. A single click on a tree row opens the file
# as a glance: the tab's name paints italic and the next glance in that
# leaf takes the tab over. A double-click keeps the tab, and so does
# the first edit, `view.keep_tab`, a pin and a drag.
#
# The italic itself cannot be read out of a text dump, so what this
# asserts is the tab COUNT — which is what the flag is FOR. (A host
# driving the file-IPC channel reads the flag itself: `status.json`'s
# pane entries carry `preview`.) The tree names the same files as the
# strip, so each count is taken with the tree folded away.
write aa.txt content-of-aa
write bb.txt content-of-bb
write cc.txt content-of-cc
open aa.txt
command tree.refresh

# Rows: the menu / palette bar is 0, the workspace header 1, then
# aa.txt 2, bb.txt 3, cc.txt 4.
expect pane "aa.txt"
# `open` is the explicit gesture — a tab of its own, never a glance.
command view.toggle_tree
expect screen contains "aa.txt"
expect screen lacks "bb.txt"
command view.toggle_tree

# One click: a glance at bb.txt, beside the tab aa.txt kept.
click 5 3
expect pane "bb.txt"
expect screen contains "content-of-bb"
command view.toggle_tree
expect screen contains "aa.txt"
expect screen contains "bb.txt"
command view.toggle_tree

# A second glance takes the preview over — two clicks, still two tabs.
click 5 4
expect pane "cc.txt"
command view.toggle_tree
expect screen contains "aa.txt"
expect screen contains "cc.txt"
expect screen lacks "bb.txt"
command view.toggle_tree

# A double-click keeps the tab, so the next glance opens beside it:
# three tabs where two clicks left two.
doubleclick 5 3
expect pane "bb.txt"
click 5 4
expect pane "cc.txt"
command view.toggle_tree
expect screen contains "aa.txt"
expect screen contains "bb.txt"
expect screen contains "cc.txt"
command view.toggle_tree

# `view.keep_tab` does by hand what the double-click does: the glance
# at aa.txt lands on the tab already open, so glance at nothing new —
# keep cc.txt (the live preview) and watch the next glance open a
# fourth tab instead of taking it over.
command view.keep_tab
write dd.txt content-of-dd
command tree.refresh
click 5 5
expect pane "dd.txt"
command view.toggle_tree
expect screen contains "cc.txt"
expect screen contains "dd.txt"
command view.toggle_tree

# The first edit keeps the tab too. Typing the edit back out makes the
# buffer clean again — but the edit already kept the tab, so the next
# glance opens beside dd.txt instead of taking it over. (A dirty tab is
# refused on its own account, which is why this asserts on a clean one.)
type Z
expect dirty true
key backspace
expect dirty false
write ee.txt content-of-ee
command tree.refresh
click 5 6
expect pane "ee.txt"
command view.toggle_tree
expect screen contains "dd.txt"
expect screen contains "ee.txt"
